.TH FEUPDATEENV 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feupdateenv \- restore floating-point environment and re-raise saved exceptions
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.BI "int feupdateenv(const fenv_t *" envp );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR feupdateenv ()
function performs the following three steps in order:
.IP 1. 4
Saves the set of currently raised floating-point exception flags.
.IP 2. 4
Restores the floating-point environment (rounding mode, exception masks, and
exception flags) from the object pointed to by
.IR envp ,
as if by calling
.BR fesetenv (3).
.IP 3. 4
Re-raises the exceptions saved in step 1 by calling
.BR feraiseexcept (3)
with those flags.
.PP
This makes
.BR feupdateenv ()
the counterpart of
.BR feholdexcept (3)
in the standard save-compute-restore pattern.
Any exceptions raised during the isolated computation are not lost; they are
merged back into the restored environment.
If the restored environment has the corresponding exception traps enabled, the
re-raised exceptions may generate a signal (SIGFPE).
.PP
The argument
.I envp
must point to an object previously filled by
.BR fegetenv (3)
or
.BR feholdexcept (3).
Passing
.B FE_DFL_ENV
as
.I envp
is also valid; it restores the default environment and then re-raises the saved
exceptions in that environment.
.PP
Use
.BR fesetenv (3)
instead of
.BR feupdateenv ()
if the exceptions raised during the computation should be silently discarded
rather than propagated.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR feupdateenv ()
returns zero on success.
A non-zero return value indicates failure (for example, if
.I envp
is invalid).
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
The function does not set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example shows the complete
.BR feholdexcept ()
\(em compute \(em
.BR feupdateenv ()
pattern for performing a block of floating-point operations in isolation:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

/*
 * Compute log(x) in an isolated FP environment.
 * Any exceptions raised are merged back into the caller's environment.
 */
double isolated_log(double x)
{
    fenv_t saved_env;

    /* Save current environment; clear all flags; mask all traps. */
    feholdexcept(&saved_env);

    double result = log(x);

    /*
     * Optionally inspect which exceptions occurred before restoring.
     * (These are the flags raised only within this block.)
     */
    if (fetestexcept(FE_DIVBYZERO))
        fprintf(stderr, "isolated_log: log(0) -> -Inf\\n");
    if (fetestexcept(FE_INVALID))
        fprintf(stderr, "isolated_log: log of negative number\\n");

    /*
     * Restore the caller's environment and re-raise any new exceptions
     * via feraiseexcept(), so the caller sees them.
     */
    feupdateenv(&saved_env);

    return result;
}

int main(void)
{
    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);

    double r = isolated_log(0.0);   /* raises FE_DIVBYZERO */
    printf("log(0.0) = %g\\n", r);

    /* FE_DIVBYZERO has been propagated back to the caller. */
    if (fetestexcept(FE_DIVBYZERO))
        printf("FE_DIVBYZERO is set in the caller.\\n");

    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR feholdexcept (3),
.BR fegetenv (3)
